  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(\\theta )}{{x}^{\u2032}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(\\theta )=-{csc}^{2}\u2061\\theta ,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{0}{-{csc}^{2}\u2061\\theta }=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(0)}{-{csc}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-{csc}^{2}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
